[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me think about the mathematics.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA C*-algebra A is simple if it has no nontrivial closed two-sided ideals.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe irreducible representations of A correspond (via the Dauns-Hofmann theorem /[0m[38;2;124;124;124mstandard C*-algebra theory) to the primitive ideals: the kernel of an [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mirreducible representation π is a primitive ideal, and every primitive ideal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124marises this way.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe property "all irreducible representations are faithful" means: for every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mirreducible representation π: A → B(H_π), ker(π) = {0}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the set of primitive ideals is just {0}.[0m

